{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (days 1–10): model is slightly high on average (~0–0.4) and tends to underreact to short up-moves; apply a small downward bias unless momentum is strongly positive.",
    "Mid-horizon (days 11–22): model is consistently too high during drawdowns (notably around the dip to ~64–66); increase mean-reversion strength downward (pull forecasts toward recent spot/5–10d mean) when the last 3–7 days are falling.",
    "Late horizon (days 23–30): model systematically misses upside breakouts and trend continuation (true rallies to ~71–73 while model stays ~66–69); add a trend-following uplift that grows with horizon when price is above the 20–30d mean and higher highs are forming.",
    "Turning points: model lags regime shifts—overestimates after peaks and underestimates after troughs; use a conditional correction: if recent slope changes sign (e.g., 3d vs 10d momentum), damp prior-trend forecasts and re-anchor to spot + reduced drift.",
    "Volatility spikes: model path is too smooth (range compressed); widen forecast distribution by increasing sensitivity to recent realized volatility (e.g., scale deviations from spot by a factor linked to 10d vol), especially beyond day 15."
  ]
}
